{ The second game of the match brought us a decisive result. Tan Zhongyi took advantage of the white pieces today and opened the score against Ju Wenjun. }
1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 e5 3. Nf3 Nc6 { Today we do see the Four Knight Variation on the board after Ju avoided it yesterday, but this time in the English Opening. } 4. g3 d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. Bg2 Bc5 (6... Nb6 { is the main move, when play usually continues with } 7. O-O Be7 8. d3 O-O 9. Be3 Be6 10. Rc1) 7. O-O O-O 8. d3 h6 9. Nxd5 Qxd5 10. Bd2 Qd6 11. Bc3 Qe7 { The first new move of the game. Since the white knight is usually headed to d2, it makes sense to remove the Queen from a future attack with Ne4 } (11... Rd8 { is more common here } 12. Nd2 Be6 { with a balanced position. White has many good moves here. Qa4, Rc1 and Ne4 are all possible. }) 12. Nd2 Nd4 13. e3 Nb5 14. a4 Nxc3 15. bxc3 { Black got the bishop pair, but this is not a significant advantage here. White's center looks quite strong and, as we will see in the game, the c5 bishop lacks square. White will manage to soon trade it off for the knight. } 15... c6 { Shutting down the bishop on g2 and preparing to develop the light squared bishop } 16. a5 { A strong move, adding pressure on b7 } 16... Bd7 (16... Be6 { , White is ready to create some weaknesses with a timely a5-a6. For example: } 17. d4 exd4 18. exd4 Bd6 19. a6 $14) 17. Qb3 (17. d4 { idea can be met by } 17... Bd6 18. a6 b5!) 17... Rab8 18. Ne4 Bd6 19. Nxd6 Qxd6 20. d4 { White traded Black's dark squared bishop and opens up the center. The position is equal, but it feels that Tan is slightly more active. } 20... b6 { Getting rid of the weak b7 pawn } 21. axb6 axb6 22. Ra7 Be6 23. Qa4 Rfc8 24. Rb1 { Black's position is perfectly fine, but the white rook on a7, the slight pressure on the c6 and b6 pawns and the very big pressure on the clock that Tan was putting on Ju here are important in practice. It's worth noting that white had exactly 30 mins more on her clock than her opponent in this moment! } 24... Bd5 25. dxe5 Qxe5 26. Qd4 { precise play, to avoid keeping a weak pawn on c3 and allowing black a potential passed pawn on the queenside. } 26... Qxd4 (26... Qe6 27. Bxd5 Qxd5 28. Qxd5 cxd5 { would give black another weakness that white can target } 29. Rb3 { followed by Rd7 looks like potential trouble }) 27. exd4 Bxg2 28. Kxg2 b5 29. Re1 Ra8 30. Ree7 Rxa7 31. Rxa7 { At this point, the game seemed to be headed for another draw } 31... c5 { still drawish, but invites unwanted complications. Now each side will get a passed pawn and black needs to be more precise. } (31... Re8 32. Kf3 { followed by } 32... Re6 { would've been easier. The black rook is more active on e6 and defends the only weakness on c6. The white king is cut off and can't make it to the queenside, to add more pressure to this pawn. Black's next moves could be g6, h5 and Kg7 }) 32. Kf3 cxd4 33. cxd4 Kf8 34. Rb7 { this pawn already looks more difficult to defend than white's d4 pawn. Black needs to find active play, which Ju immediately goes for. } 34... Rc3+ 35. Ke4 Rc2 { creating counter-attacks on white's kingside pawns. } 36. Ke3 Rc3+ 37. Kd2 Rf3 38. Ke2 Rf5 { This defends the b pawn and controls the d5 square preventing white's pawn from advancing, for now. However, the Rook looks slightly misplaced here. } (38... Rb3 { was the alternative. It looks like the right thing to do, as it also keeps the white king cut off. However, the d pawn is allowed to advance and that can look scary. With little time on the clock, it was difficult for Ju to work out all the lines and defensive resources. } 39. d5 Ke8 { should hold the draw, though. It's highly important that the white king is kept passive here. } 40. h4 h5 41. Kd2 { trying to go to c2 and free the King can be met by Rf3, with pressure on f2: } (41. d6 Ra3 42. Rxb5 g6 43. Rb8+ (43. Rb7 Ra6) 43... Kd7 44. Rb7+ Ke6 { seems simplest, keeping f7 alive } 45. d7 (45. Re7+ Kf6 { there is no d7 and Ra6 is the threat }) 45... Ra8 { followed by Rd8 and all is well }) 41... g6 42. Kc2 Rf3 43. Rxb5 Rxf2+ 44. Kd3 { now, when the white king advances, black can clear out the kingside pawns and create counterplay. } 44... Rf3+ 45. Kd4 Rxg3) 39. Ke3 h5 40. f4 Ke8? { This is the decisive mistake, but Black's position was already tough. In practice, this position should be very difficult to defend. Black needs to find only moves and that's something only engines excel at. } (40... b4 { was the last chance Black had to keep the fight going. This pawn seems to be lost either way, but the idea of this move is to bring the rook into play. Otherwise, it gets stuck on f5 and black remains without any counterplay. } 41. Rxb4 Ra5 42. Ke4 f5+! { another precise move, otherwise White gets to play d5 and further activate the King } 43. Kd3 Kf7 44. Kc4 Ra2 { Black gets her counterplay now } 45. h4 Rc2+! 46. Kd5 (46. Kd3 Rg2) 46... Rc3 { winning the g3 pawn }) 41. Ke4 Rf6 42. Rxb5 g6 43. d5 Ra6 { The rook finally gets out, but White's pieces are already too active. Tan's play is remarkable once she got a winning position. } 44. Ke5 f6+ 45. Kd4 Ra2 46. Kc5 Rxh2 47. Rb8+ (47. Rb7 { is slow } 47... h4 { and black is a tempo ahead compared to the game }) 47... Kd7 48. Rb7+ Kc8 { very precise play, getting her Rook on the 7th rank with tempo } (48... Ke8 49. Kd6 g5 (49... Re2 50. Rg7 $18) 50. Ke6! $18) 49. Rg7 g5 50. f5! (50. fxg5? fxg5 51. Rxg5 h4 52. g4 (52. gxh4 Rxh4 { is a draw }) 52... h3 53. Rh5 Rg2 { would also end in a draw }) 50... h4 51. gxh4 gxh4 52. Rh7 h3 53. Kd6! (53. d6? { allowing the black King to stay in front of the passed pawn } 53... Kd8 54. Kc6 Rc2+) 53... Kb8 (53... Rf2 54. Rxh3 Rxf5 55. Kc6 Kb8 56. d6 Rf1 57. Rh8+ Ka7 58. d7 $18) 54. Kd7 Kb7 55. d6 Kb6 56. Kd8 Rh1 57. d7 h2 58. Rh3 Kb7 59. Rb3+ Ka7 60. Rb2 { White prepares the bridge, but also wants to keep an eye on the h2 pawn } 60... Ka6 61. Re2 Kb7 62. Ke7 { 1-0 White wins. } 1-0


