{ We entered round 3 with Tan leading on the scoreboard. It was a very long grind, but, after more than 6 hours of play, Ju managed to level the score! }
1. e4 { Ju went for 1.e4 again and for a moment it seemed like we might have the same line as in round 1 on the board. } 1... c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. c4 { Ju goes for a different line on move 3, probably hoping to surprise her opponent. } 3... Nc6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Bb4+ { One thing that has become clear to me so far in this match is that Tan seems to be extremely well-prepared. Ju doesn't usually open with e4 and I couldn't find any previous games of her in this line, so it's unlikely that Tan was expecting to see this position on the board. However, she played very fast and went for one of the less popular continuations, 5... Bb4. } 6. Nc3 Nge7 7. Be2 { At this point, it almost felt like Tan had caught her opponent off-guard. Ju spent around 5 minutes here and went for the more solid approach. } (7. Nb5 { looked critical, but the position after } 7... O-O 8. a3 Bxc3+ 9. Nxc3 f5 { becomes messy and certainly riskier. }) 7... d5 8. exd5 exd5 9. Nxc6 bxc6 10. O-O O-O 11. cxd5 cxd5 { White got a comfortable position out of the opening. The d5 pawn is weak and she can try to put pressure on it. } 12. Bf3 { immediately eyeing the pawn } (12. Bf4 { , developing, was another possibility, perhaps more testing for Black. White can follow up with Rc1 and Nb5, while Black's development isn't so easy. The knight could eventually go to d4, but Black will need to defend some threats on c7 as well. } { [%cal Ga1c1,Gc3b5,Gb5d4,Gb5c7] }) 12... Be6 13. Bg5 { threatening to win the d5 pawn } (13. Bf4 { comes with less strength now, as Black is ready to play } 13... Rc8) (13. Be3 Qd7 { Black also seems fine after Rac8 }) 13... Bxc3 14. bxc3 Rc8 { It looked like Tan had again equalised comfortably. The d5 and a7 pawns are weak, but Black can target c3 and a2 in return. Ideally, she would like to trade her two weaknesses for White's. } 15. Qa4 h6 (15... Rxc3 16. Qxa7 Rc7 17. Qd4 { might be a bit more difficult to defend for Black. The a pawn can advance and there is still a lot of pressure on d5. }) 16. Bd2 a5 17. Rfe1 Rc4 18. Qa3 Nf5 { Black is ready to push d5-d4 } 19. Be2 Rc8 20. Rad1 Qd6 (20... d4 { seems critical, although visually it looks more dangerous to go for with a white Rook on d1 } 21. cxd4 Nxd4 22. Qxa5 (22. Bxa5?? { doesn't work in view of } 22... Nxe2+ 23. Kf1 Qg5 24. Rxe2 Bc4 $19) 22... Nxe2+ 23. Rxe2 Rc2 { Black is getting the a2 pawn back. The immediate threat is Qxa5, since Bd2 is now pinned. }) 21. Qxa5 Ra8 22. Qb4 Rxa2 23. Bf4 { White is more active here compared to the previous line } 23... Qc6 24. Bb5 Qb6 25. Re2 { protecting f2 } 25... Rfa8 (25... Rxe2 26. Bxe2 Qxb4 27. cxb4 { is what White is waiting for. The two bishops and the passed pawn on the b file will be decisive. }) 26. Bd3 Ra1 { Black did not blunder the Queen } 27. Ree1 (27. Qxb6 { runs into } 27... Rxd1+ 28. Re1 Rxe1+ 29. Bf1 Raa1 $19) 27... Rxd1 28. Rxd1 Qd8 { again, avoiding the trade into a worse endgame } 29. h3 Qf6 30. Re1 { preparing Be5 } 30... Rc8 31. Be5 Qg5 32. Ba6 Ra8 33. Bf4 Qf6 34. Bb7 Rd8 35. Bc7 { This position is not easy to play for Black. The white bishops are very strong and the d5 pawn is under fire. } 35... Rf8 (35... Rd7 { was the way to go. However, the position is still tough for Tan } 36. Be5 Qg5 (36... Qd8 37. Ra1)) 36. Rd1 Qg5 { Tan finds the best defense in this difficult position } 37. Bxd5 { White wins a pawn, but after } 37... Ne3! { will trade into an endgame with opposite colored bishops, which looked easier to hold than it turned out to be. } 38. h4! { Precise play from Ju as well } (38. fxe3 Bxd5 39. e4 Qe3+ 40. Kh2 Bxe4 { or Qxe4 is much more comfortable for Black than the game }) 38... Qg4 39. Qxg4 Bxg4 40. Rd3 Nxd5 41. Rxd5 { White has emerged a pawn up, but the game seemed to be headed for a draw either way } 41... Ra8 42. Bb6 Be6 43. Rd6 Kf8 { Both players start improving their pieces } 44. Bd4 Ke7 45. Rb6 g6 { Tan prefers to defend by placing her pawns on light squares, but, as we will see in the game, this will allow an entry path for the white King. } (45... f6 { was the other set-up. } 46. Rb7+ Kf8 47. Kh2 Ra4 { making sure that the c pawn will not advance easily, but also keeping the Rook active } 48. Kg3 Bd5 { and now waiting with the Bishop on c4 and d5 }) 46. Kh2 h5 47. Kg3 Ra5 48. Kf4 { from f4, the King could go either to d3 and help the c pawn advance or to g5-h6 and eventually g7. } 48... Kd7 49. g3 Rf5+ { Black's position is far from easy } (49... Ra2 { for example, then she can easily get in trouble } 50. Ke5 Ra5+ 51. Kf6 Rf5+ 52. Kg7 Ra5 53. Be3! { and the idea is to sacrifice the exchange on f7: } 53... Ra3 54. Rb7+ Kc8 (54... Ke8 55. Bg5 $18) 55. Rxf7 Bxf7 56. Kxf7 $18 { This is just a line to illustrate all the ideas that Black had to watch out for in this position }) 50. Ke3 Ra5 51. Rb4 { Preparing c4 } 51... Bd5 52. Bb6 (52. c4 Bc6 53. c5 Ra3+ 54. Kf4 Ra4 { and White is forced to trade Rooks } 55. Rxa4 Bxa4 { This endgame would be an easy draw } 56. Ke5 Kc6 57. Kf6 Bb3 58. Kg5 Be6) 52... Ra3 53. Kd4 { Ju is playing the endgame with a lot of patience. Black's position is difficult, as we have seen, and there are many opportunities to go wrong. Adding the time-trouble to this, she has very good practical chances to win it. } 53... Be6 54. Bc5 Ra2 55. Kd3 Bf5+ 56. Ke3 Be6 57. Bd4 { Not much has happened over the last few moves, but we have reached a position where White is threatening one more time to bring her King to f4 and g5. } 57... Kc6 (57... Ra5 { could now be met with } 58. c4 { and some progress has been made }) 58. c4 Kc7 59. c5 Bd5?! (59... Bd7! { was the right way to bring this Bishop to c6. Why? Because now the defensive idea with Ra4 works! } 60. Kf4 Ra4 { and white does not make it with her King to the kingside. }) 60. Kf4 Bc6? { Tan tries to use the Rook exchange idea one more time to defend against the entry of the white King, but this time she goes wrong. } (60... Re2 { was the only move here } 61. Kg5 Re4 62. Kf6 Re6+ 63. Kg7 Re4 { was the idea. White doesn't seem to be able to reach the right position to sacrifice on f7, although she will surely try with } 64. Ra4 { for example } 64... Kb7 $14) 61. Kg5! { the right path to f6 } (61. Ke5? { would not work due to } 61... Ra4 { and Black forces the Rook trade again. The point is that } 62. Rb2 { is met with } 62... f6+ { winning the Bishop on d4. }) 61... Ra4 62. Be5+ { This is probably what Tan missed } 62... Kd7 63. Rb2 { White is not
interested in trading rooks! } 63... Ke6 64. Bf4 Rc4 65. Be3 Ke5 (65... Rg4+ { doesn't help } 66. Kh6 Rc4 (66... Kf6 { preventing the entry on g7 never works due to } 67. Rb6 $18) 67. Kg7 Ra4 68. Rb6 $18) 66. Rb8 Bd7 67. Rd8 Be6 68. Rd6 { threatening Bf4+ followed by c6 } 68... Rc2 (68... Bd5 69. Kh6 Rc2 70. Kg7 Rc4 71. Rxd5+ { is the idea } 71... Kxd5 72. Kxf7 $18) 69. Kh6 (69. Bf4+ Ke4 70. c6 { also worked }) 69... Kf6 70. Rd1 Rc4 71. Rd6 Rc2 72. Bd4+ Kf5 (72... Ke7 73. Kg7 Bd7 { we already know how white can play here - } 74. Rxd7+ Kxd7 75. Kxf7 $18 Rc4 76. Be3 Rg4 77. Bf4 { traps the Rook }) 73. f3 (73. c6?! { allows } 73... Ke4 74. Bb6 Ke5 { and Black is back in the game }) 73... Rc4 74. c6 Rc2 75. Kg7 g5 (75... Rc1 76. Kf8 { followed by Ke8-d8 is white's plan }) 76. hxg5 Kxg5 77. Bb6 (77. Bf6+ Kf5 78. Rd4 { was a nice mating net } { [%cal Gd4f4] } 78... Rc4 79. g4+) 77... Kf5 78. c7 Rc3 79. Ba5 Rc5 80. Ra6 Rc4 81. Kf8 Bd5 82. Ke7 h4 83. Rf6+ Ke5 (83... Kg5 { leads to checkmate after } 84. Bd2+ Kh5 85. Rh6#) 84. gxh4 Be6 85. h5 Rc5 86. h6 Bc8 87. h7 { A nice grind by Ju, who bounces back immediately after the loss in round 2. } 1-0


