{ Today we had an exciting game, where Tan seemed to have the upper hand in the endgame. However, Ju defended very well and the game ended peacefully. }
1. c4 { Once again, the English Opening. So far, the two players stay consistent with their opening choices in the match. } 1... e6 2. Nc3 Bb4 { Ju goes for a slightly offbeat opening, most likely hoping to surprise her opponent. } 3. Qb3 { The critical line. Again, we see that Tan is very well-prepared. } 3... Ba5 (3... Nc6 { is the most popular move here, but the game move is the engine's top choice and has been tried by a few strong players recently. }) 4. g3 c5 5. Bg2 Nc6 6. e3 (6. Nf3 Nge7 7. O-O O-O 8. Rd1 d5 9. d4 dxc4 10. Qxc4 cxd4 11. Bf4 Bb6 12. Nb5 Nf5 13. e3 Bd7 { Miles,A (2585) -Oll,L (2560) Sydney 1991 }) 6... Nge7 { Ju spent a long time on this move, probably deciding on the best way to place her pieces. } 7. Nge2 O-O 8. O-O a6 { threatening to go ...b5 } 9. d4 cxd4 10. exd4 d5 11. cxd5 exd5 12. Be3 (12. Nxd5 Nxd5 13. Bxd5 { but she probably didn't like Black's piece activity after } 13... Nb4 14. Bg2 Be6 15. Qd1 Re8 { [%cal Ge6c4] }) 12... Bxc3 13. Nxc3 Na5 14. Qb4 Nac6 15. Qc5 b6 (15... Be6 { immediately was probably better. Rc8 will follow and the White Queen will still have to reatreat to a3, but without weakening the queenside pawns. }) 16. Qa3 Be6 17. Rac1 Nf5 18. Bf4 { Tan doesn't mind losing the d4 pawn, since d5 will most likely fall soon. She prefers to play for activity. } (18. Ne2 { looks strong, with the idea of copying Black's set-up in the future - the knight could go the f4, to attack the d5 pawn } 18... Na5 19. b3 $16 { to stop Nc4 }) 18... Nfxd4 19. Rfd1 { A difficult position for Ju in practice - everything seems to be hanging. Be3 is an annoying threat } 19... Nb5 { A logical human reaction. Ju prefers to return the pawn and simplify the position than to keep the knight on d4 always hanging, the pin on the d file and on the long diagonal. Unfortunately, this gives white a big advantage } (19... Re8 { is its main choice, claiming that nothing is happening. But this kind of choice is very difficult for a human in practice } 20. Be3 Qf6) 20. Nxb5 axb5 21. Qb3 Ne7 22. Qxb5 Rxa2 { After all the trades, white remains with the bishop pair and very active pieces. } 23. Bc7 { will get back the b pawn and at this point the game seemed to go very much in Tan's favor } 23... Qa8 24. Bd6? (24. Qxb6 { was the move everyone was expecting here. As she admitted in the press conference, Tan thought that she could capture the pawn anyway after the game move. }) 24... Ra5 25. Qb4 (25. Qxb6 { Black had the move } 25... Nc8 { trading the dark squared bishop for the knight }) 25... Ra4 26. Qb3 Ra7 27. Rc7 Rxc7 28. Bxc7 Qc6 29. Qxb6 { The pawn is still lost, but Black is much more active here. At this point, the game looked an awful lot like the position Ju won yesterday. White has the bishop pair and black's d5 pawn is a target. The only difference is that Tan has her pawn on the b file. } 29... Qc2 30. Bf3 Bf5 { the bishop is headed to e4, to get rid of white's bishop pair. } (30... d4! { was a very nice resource here that Ju also considered during the game. It looked a bit risky to her, so she went for Bf5 instead, which had the same idea. The tricky point is that } 31. Rxd4?? { loses due to } (31. Bf4 { would be met by } 31... Bd5) 31... Nd5!! 32. Bxd5 Qb1+ 33. Kg2 Bxd5+ 34. Rxd5 Qe4+ $19) 31. Bf4 Be4 32. Rc1 Qd3 33. Bxe4 Qxe4 (33... dxe4 { was the better choice. The not so obvious point is to vacate the d5 square for the knight. Without this square, the knight remains slightly passive } 34. Qe3 Qxe3 35. Bxe3 Nd5 { might be easier for Black than the game }) 34. Qe3! { very good strategic understanding by Tan. The bishops are generally better than the knights in the endgame and this move forces the trade of queens. White's threat is to trade queens and follow up with Bd6, which would win the knight. } 34... Qxe3?! (34... Rd8! { was the solution to Black's problems. The point is once again to open up the d5 square for the knight } 35. Qxe4 dxe4 { [%cal Ge7d5] }) 35. Bxe3 f6 36. Rc7 Nf5 37. Bc5 Rd8 38. Kf1 Nd6 39. b4 (39. Ke2 Ne4 40. Kd3 Nxc5+ 41. Rxc5 Rb8 42. Kc2 $18) 39... Ne4 40. Bb6 (40. Ke2 { was still working, although black gets to play } 40... d4 { and the game becomes complicated }) 40... Rb8 41. Ba5 Rb5 { very good defense by Ju! the rook can't be chased away from b5 and now the white bishop is stuck on a5. } 42. Ke2 Nd6 43. Ra7 Nc4 44. Kd3 Ne5+ { Black has equalized and the game seemed to be headed for a draw } 45. Kc2 d4 46. Kb3 Rd5 { Whenever she gets the choice between pushing a pawn and improving a piece, Ju chooses the piece! } 47. Bb6 d3 48. Ra1 Nf3 49. Rd1 Kf7 50. Kc3 d2 51. Be3 Ke6 52. Bxd2 Nxh2 53. Re1+ Kd7 { the King is misplaced on d7 } (53... Kf5 { , an idea that Ju eventually went for in the game, was the better choice here. She probably didn't want to allow } 54. Re7 { but something similar to the game could be played } 54... g5 55. Rxh7 Ng4 56. f3 Ne5 57. f4 Nd3 58. fxg5 Nxb4!) 54. Be3 Ng4 55. Bc5 Ne5 56. Ra1 (56. f4! { was Tan's last chance to play for a win } 56... Nc6 57. Kc4 Rd2 58. Ra1 Ke6 { is no longer possible due to } 59. Ra6 $18) 56... Ke6! { activating the King } 57. f4 Nd7 58. Re1+ Kf5 59. Kc4 Rd2 60. Re7 Nxc5! { very precise. Everything else allows white to keep pressing for a win } 61. bxc5 g5 62. fxg5 fxg5 63. Rxh7 Rc2+ 64. Kd4 Rd2+ 65. Kc4 Rc2+ 66. Kb5 Rc3 (66... Kg4 67. Rg7 { and } 67... Rc3 { is needed anyway }) 67. c6 Rxg3 68. c7 Rc3 69. Kb6 Kf4 70. Rf7+ Ke4! (70... Kg3?? { in order to make a draw in the Rook vs pawn endgame, the black King needs to be able to shoulder the white King. From g3 it is unable to do that and the endgame would be lost } 71. Rf6 { with the same Rc6 threat as in the game } 71... Rxc7 72. Kxc7 g4 73. Kd6 Kh2 74. Ke5 g3 75. Kf4 g2 76. Rh6+ Kg1 77. Kg3 { and white wins }) 71. Rg7 Kf4 72. Rg6 { Rc6 is the threat } 72... Rxc7 73. Kxc7 g4 74. Kd6 g3 { Now the white King doesn't have the e5 square available and can't get close to the g pawn } 75. Kd5 Kf3 76. Kd4 g2 77. Kd3 Kf2 78. Rf6+ Ke1 79. Rg6 Kf2 80. Rxg2+ Kxg2 { 1/2-1/2 } 1/2-1/2


