1. e4 { Until now, both players have stayed consistent with their 1st move choice. } 1... c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 { We have the same position as in game 1, but today Tan chose to deviate from that game with her next move } 4... a6 { entering Sicilian Kan territory } 5. Bd3 Bc5 6. Nb3 Ba7 7. O-O Ne7 8. c4 Nbc6 9. Nc3 Ne5 { Tan played the opening very fast and again went for a sideline. There are only 3 games with this move in my database. } (9... O-O { is the main move } 10. Qe2 d5 11. exd5 exd5) 10. c5 { Ju thought for about 6 minutes here and went for a very logical continuation. The idea is to try to fix black's pawn on d7 and hinder her opponent's development as much as possible. } 10... N7g6 (10... Nxd3 11. Qxd3 { then she will have problems with the d6 square. }) (10... b6 { is the normal reaction, to trade this "thorn" in Black's position } 11. Be3 (11. cxb6 Bxb6 { looks comfrotable for Black }) 11... O-O) (10... d6?! { doesn't work, as Black can't recapture the pawn with the Queen } 11. cxd6 Qxd6?? 12. Bb5+) 11. Be2 { this move prepares f4 } (11. f4 { right away, Black can win a tempo by trading on d3 } 11... Nxd3 12. Qxd3 b6 { White keeps an edge, but Black is a bit more comfortable now the knight has been traded off }) 11... b5? { Gaining an outpost for her knight on c4, but this move looks rather strange. Black should try to undermine the c5 pawn as soon as possible. } (11... b6 { was again the way to go. The move } 12. f4 { is most likely what looked very uncomfortable for Tan, but things shouldn't be so bad after } 12... Nc6 13. Be3 { White has an advantage of course due to her better development and piece cooperation, but Black's prospects look a bit better than in the game } 13... bxc5 14. Bxc5 Bxc5+ 15. Nxc5 d6 16. N5a4 $14) 12. f4 Nc4 13. a4 { another logical reaction, meant to undermine the knight on c4 } 13... b4 14. Bxc4 bxc3 { At this point it looked like we wouldn't have a very long game. White's development advantage and the prospects of f5-f6 on the kingside in case of 0-0 look scary. } 15. bxc3 (15. Qd4! { would've been really testing for Black } 15... cxb2 { brings the bishop on the long diagonal, preventing Black from castling any time soon } (15... O-O { can be met by } 16. f5 $18 { the knight would like to return to safety on e7, but f6 is coming. }) 16. Bxb2 f6 17. f5 $18 { looks crushing as well }) 15... Bb7 16. Qe2 { a bit modest } (16. Qd4 { looked tempting again, with the same f5-f6 idea against 0-0 } 16... d6 { doesn't really work, since White can play } 17. Qxg7 { and threaten f5 }) 16... O-O 17. Bd3 { probably preparing Be3 } 17... f5 { trying to open up the bishop on b7 and perhaps get some counterplay. However, the engine points out that e5! was in fact the right pawn break. } (17... Qc7 18. Be3 e5! 19. f5 Nf4 { is the idea. } 20. Bxf4 exf4 { without the dark squared bishop, black will most likely win the c5 pawn soon and get good play against White's queenside pawns }) 18. exf5 exf5 19. Be3 (19. Bxa6?? { loses after } 19... Bxa6 20. Qxa6 Bxc5+ $19) 19... Qc7 20. Bxa6 Rfe8 (20... Bxa6 21. Qxa6 Bxc5?? 22. Qc4+ $18) 21. Bxb7 Qxb7 22. Qc4+ { Black has no compensation at all for the missing two pawns } 22... Kh8 23. Bd4 { now Qf7 is a threat } { [%csl Rf5,Rg7][%cal Gc4f7] } 23... Re6 24. h3 Rae8 { Both R2 and Bb8 are threats } (24... Bb8 { Black would love to force white to play g3 and weaken her King. Then, she could get counterplay on the kingside with Rg6 or h6 ideas. But } 25. Qb5! { would be very strong } (25. g3?? Nxf4 { and black is back in the game! White can't recapture, as } 26. gxf4 Rg6+ { would be game over }) 25... Qc7 { as soon as the black Queen leaves the long diagonal, } (25... Qd5 { can be met with } 26. c4) (25... Qxb5 26. axb5 Rxa1 27. Rxa1 $18 { should be easy for White }) 26. g3 { is safe to play }) 25. Rf2 Bb8 26. Raf1 { and everything is protected } 26... Nxf4 { If Black doesn't try anything, she will lose this slowly, but surely. White can improve her position with Qb5, maybe Nd2-c4 and eventually advance her pawns } 27. Rxf4 Bxf4 28. Rxf4 Re1+ 29. Rf1 { the only move to keep the advantage, although not a very difficult one } (29. Kh2 Qc7 { looks bad for White }) (29. Kf2 { doesn't look like a real option either - what is the King doing on f2 when Black is ready to invade with both her Rooks and the Queen? }) 29... Rxf1+ 30. Kxf1 Qe4 31. Kg1 { getting back to safety } 31... Qb1+ 32. Kh2 Re1 { Black has a sneaky threat - f4! and White is in very big trouble } 33. Qd5 { threatening checkmate on a8 } 33... h6 34. Nd2 { there's no time for ...f4 } 34... Rh1+ (34... Qc1 35. Qxf5! Qxd2 36. Qf8+ Kh7 37. Qxg7#) 35. Kg3 Qe1+ 36. Bf2 Qe2 { again, f4 is coming } 37. Qd4 Rd1 38. Be3 Kh7 39. a5 { Now that everything is protected Ju starts advancing her passed pawn } 39... Re1 40. Bf2 Rd1 41. Be3 Re1 42. Nc4 Ra1 43. Nd6 Ra2 44. Bf2 Qe6 { defending f5 } 45. Qf4 Kg6 46. Kh2 Qf6 47. Nc4 { White is winning in many ways here. She is now threatening Ne5 } 47... Kh7 48. Bd4 Qe6 49. Nd6 Qg6 (49... Kg6 { no longer works in view of } 50. Qg3+) 50. Qf3 f4 51. a6 Rxa6 52. Qxf4 Ra2 53. Qf3 Qe6 54. Nf5 g5 55. Ne3 { the long diagonal is weak and the knight is headed to f6 via d5 or g4 } 55... d6 56. c6 Ra8 57. Nd5 Rc8 58. Qd3+ Kg8 59. Ne7+ { a nice tactic to finish the game } 1-0


