1. c4 { Tan remains faithful to the English Opening in this match } 1... Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. b3 { White sidesteps the well-known paths of 3.g3 of 3.Nc3 and chooses a line that has become fashionable nowadays. } 3... d5 4. Bb2 c5 { Ju takes the principled approach and occupies the center with her pawns. 5...d4 has become a threat, so White needs to do something about it } 5. cxd5 (5. e3 { is also possible and transposes to a set-up that Vincent Keymer has popularized in the recent years } 5... Nc6 6. cxd5 exd5 { and now } 7. Bb5 { and 7.Be2 are the main choices for White }) 5... exd5 6. g3 { going for a double fianchetto set-up } 6... Nc6 7. Bg2 d4 { gaining more space and shutting down the bishop on b2 } 8. O-O Be7 9. Na3 { Black has a space advantage and white's position might seem cramped right now, but the position is very rich in ideas. White's queenside knight is headed to c4 and she will challenge black's strong center with e3. The bishop on g2 is very active and can become dangerous later in the game. } 9... O-O 10. e3 dxe3 { keeping the central tension and developing the light squared bishop to f5 or g4 was the other option } (10... Bf5 11. exd4 cxd4 12. Nc4 { leads to an interesting position. The pawn on d4 gives Black a space advantage. At the same time, it can become a weakness. White is already putting pressure on it and would like to follow up with Ne5 and trade its main defender soon. }) 11. dxe3 Bf5 12. Ne5 (12. Nc4 { was also normal, but perhaps Tan didn't want to allow } 12... Be4 { which would eventually trade off her strong bishop on g2 }) 12... Nxe5 13. Bxe5 Qc8 14. Qe2 { clearing the d file for one of the rooks } 14... Bh3 { a sensible decision - Black's play will be much easier without this bishop controlling the long diagonal. Moreover, Ju could try to use the weakness of the light squares around the king, as she eventually did in the game. } 15. Qf3 { it's quite common that, whenever the fianchetto bishop gets traded off, the Queen replaces it on the long diagonal. } 15... Bxg2 16. Kxg2 Qe6 17. Bb2 (17. Bxf6 Bxf6 18. Rad1) 17... Ne4 { Black's position looks very comfortable now } 18. Rfd1 Rad8 19. Nc4 b5 { the white knight seems unable to find a good square yet } 20. Ne5 f5 { creating an outpost for the knight on e4 and threatening Bf6 } 21. Nd3 { If White manages to play Nf4 and h4, everything should be okay, but... } 21... g5! { White didn't make any big mistakes, but her position got significantly worse over the last few moves. The weakness of the light squares will be very much felt after g5-g4. With the black kinght on g5 and the Queen on the long diagonal (c6 or e4), white's position should soon crumble. } 22. Ne1 { What else? c5-c4 followed by c3 was the immediate threat } 22... Bf6?! (22... g4 { still looked very promising } 23. Qe2 (23. Qf4?? Bd6 $19 { traps the queen }) 23... c4 { followed by Qc6 is very strong } (23... Qc6 { immediately also lookstempting. Perhaps the position after } 24. f3 Ng5 25. h4 { didn't look very clear to Ju })) 23. Bxf6 Qxf6 24. Rxd8 Rxd8 25. Rd1 Rxd1 26. Qxd1 { At this point it looked like the game was headed for a draw. As we will see, this is not so easy to achieve for White. } 26... Qd6! { inviting the trade into a knights endgame where Black's activity should be very uncomfortable. } 27. Qh5! { precise defense from Tan } (27. Qxd6 Nxd6 28. Kf3 Kf7 29. h3 Ke6 { only Black can win this. Ju can create a passed pawn on the queenside, while Tan is yet to find an active spot for her kinght. }) 27... Kg7 { Ju takes no risks. Her king is better placed on g7 anyway. } (27... Qd5 { would allow the white queen in } 28. Qe8+ Kg7 29. Qe7+ Kg6 30. h4 { and White has managed to create her own threats. }) 28. Qe8 a6 29. Nf3 Nf6! { a very strong move, as White was most likely planning to get her knight to e5. } 30. Qa8! { only move to stay in the game. White can't allow the black Queen to take over the long diagonal. } (30. Qc8 { loses to } 30... Qd5 31. Qc7+ Kg6 $18 { [%cal Gg5g4] } 32. Qe5 Qc6) (30. Qe5 Qc6! { and there's no stopping g4 }) 30... h6 31. h4? { After defeding so well for the last moves, Tan crumbles under the pressure. It's certainly not easy to find moves here. } (31. Qa7+ Kg6 32. Qb7 { but black still has many ideas to keep pressuring her opponent. One is to force the trade into a knights endgame with Qd5. The other is to create a passed pawn by pushing c5-c4 first. }) 31... g4 32. Qb7+ (32. Ne1 Qd5+ 33. Qxd5 Nxd5 { is very similar to the game. White is doomed to passivity, while Black will slowly improve her position. }) 32... Qd7 33. Qxd7+ Nxd7 34. Nd2 Kf6 { improving her King } 35. f4 (35. Kf1 Ke5 36. Ke2 Nf6 37. Kd3 Ne4 { forcing the trade into a winning pawn endgame is Black's plan }) 35... Ke6 36. e4 { Tan finds an active idea that allows her to create a passed pawn, but Black's advantage is too big } 36... fxe4 37. Nxe4 Kd5 38. Nf2 Nf6 39. Kf1 Kd4 40. Ke2 h5 { Ju plays this with a lot of patience } (40... c4 { is also winning, but why not have the g4 protected first, so she can move the knight when she needs it? }) 41. a4 Kc3 42. axb5 axb5 43. Nd3 Ne4 44. Ke3 Nxg3 45. Nxc5 Nf5+ 46. Ke4 Nxh4 47. Ke5 (47. f5 g3 $19) 47... Kb4 48. Ne4 Kxb3 49. Kf6 Nf3 { the h pawn is ready to advance } 50. f5 h4 51. Ke7 g3 52. f6 g2 { 0-1 Black wins. } 53. f7 { doesn't help in view of } 53... Ne5 54. f8=Q Ng6+ $19 0-1


