{ I am Petra Papp, WGM from Hungary. I am pleased to analyze the last six games of the World Championship match between Ju Wenjun and Tan Zhongyi. The match has been quite exciting so far. The outcomes of these games have predominantly been decided in the endgame phase, where the players' nerves and ability to maintain control until the end are critical. Ju Wenjun seems to be in better form and certainly has significantly more experience. In this game, Tan played exceptionally well and dominated throughout. At one point, she held a decisive advantage. Ju then sacrificed a pawn for compensation but maintained a solid position, prepared to draw in several moves. I would say the draw would be a fair result, but at one point, Tan made a bad decision 35...♗b7, and probably missed the 36.♗c5! idea, after that, it was already hard to defend. Ju's technique, solidity, and ability to maintain the balance are impressively high. }
1. e4 { My favorite move too. Ju has played in all the games during the match. } 1... c5 { Once again, we encounter the Sicilian. } 2. Nf3 Nc6 { This is the first surprise in the game. Tan had previously played 2...e6. However, halfway through the tournament, she had two rest days, which allowed her to prepare a new strategy. } (2... e6 { You can review it in the previous games. }) 3. Bb5 { The Rossolimo is a popular and fashionable opening nowadays. It’s quite solid and mainly avoids the sharp Kalashnikov and Sveshnikov openings. } (3. d4) 3... e6 (3... g6 { The other main move. }) 4. O-O Nge7 { I have to note that Tan has developed the knight to e7 twice in the match already, so she really likes this move. } 5. d4 { The third most popular move, but is quite straightforward. } (5. c3 a6 6. Ba4 b5 7. Bc2 Bb7 $132 { [%cal Ga8c8,Ge7g6] }) (5. Re1 { This is the main move. }) 5... cxd4 6. Nxd4 Qb6 { A concrete move attacks the knight on d4 while also pressuring the bishop on b5. } (6... Ng6 { Nowadays, it is more popular, but White can still secure a small, stable advantage. } 7. Be3 Be7 8. c3 O-O 9. Nd2 a6 10. Bd3 b5 11. Qh5! Bb7 12. f4 $36) 7. Nxc6! bxc6! { A correct recapture. It's quite logical to recapture towards the center, and black is also opening up the b-file for the rook. } (7... Qxb5? 8. Nd4! Qb6 9. Nc3 a6 10. Be3 $16) (7... dxc6 8. Bc4 Ng6 9. Kh1! { [%cal Gf2f4] } 9... Be7 10. f4 O-O 11. Qe2 $14 { [%cal Ga2a4,Gb1c3,Gc1e3] }) (7... Nxc6 8. Nc3 { [%cal Rc1e3] } 8... a6 9. Be3 Qc7 10. Be2 b5 11. Qd2 $14 { [%cal Ge3f4] }) 8. Bd3 { This is an interesting decision; both 8.♗e2 and 8.♗d3 have their points. If White retreats to e2 in the future, the plan is to use the bishop on f3 after playing f2-f4. On d3, the idea is to create threats along the b1-h7 diagonal. } (8. Be2!? { I prefer this move, as I believe that White has a favorable position afterwards. } 8... Ng6 9. c4 Be7 10. Nc3 O-O 11. Rb1! { A typical move that overprotects the b2 pawn and prepares for Be3. } 11... Qc7 12. Be3 c5 13. f4 Bb7 14. g3!? Rfd8 15. h4 Nf8 16. Bf3 $14) 8... Ng6 9. c4 { A standard move. White maintains control of the center and aims for more space. } 9... Be7 { The most solid and logical square for the bishop. } (9... Bd6?! 10. Nc3 O-O 11. g3 $14 { [%cal Gc3a4,Gf2f4] }) (9... Bc5 10. Nc3 { [%cal Gc3a4] } 10... Qc7 11. Na4 Bd4 12. c5 O-O 13. Re1 $14 { [%cal Gd3f1] }) 10. Nc3 O-O 11. Na4 (11. Rb1 { The main move, which involves preparing ♗e3, but Black is okay after that too. } { [%csl Gb2] } 11... Qc7 12. Be3 Rd8 13. f4 d5 14. cxd5 exd5 15. exd5 cxd5 16. Nb5 Qb7 17. Nd4 Bf6 $13 { Kamalidenova-Wagner, Bydgoszcz rapid 2023 }) 11... Qc7 12. c5 { An interesting decision that will lead to an unbalanced pawn structure. } (12. Be3!? $13 { This is a new move and a top favorite of the engine. White is set to play ♖c1 and ♘c5. The position remains well-balanced. } { [%cal Ga1c1,Ga4c5] }) 12... d5! 13. cxd6 Bxd6 14. Qh5?! $146 { This is the game's first new move. With precise moves, Black could take the initiative. Black could have pursued two promising ideas. } (14. g3!? { Almost everyone has played this move. The point is to control the f4 square. However, I believe that Black has a solid position. } { [%csl Rf4,Rh4] } 14... c5! 15. Be3 c4 16. Rc1 Ba6! 17. Be2 Rfd8 18. Qc2 Rac8 19. f4 $13) 14... Rb8 { A logical move as well. } (14... c5!? { [%cal Gc5c4,Gc8d7] } 15. Be3 (15. b3?! Bd7 16. Nb2 Be5 17. Rb1 Bc6 18. Nc4 Bd4 $15) 15... c4 16. Be2 (16. Rac1 Ba6! 17. b3 Nf4! 18. Bxf4 Bxf4 19. Rc2 g6 20. Qh4 Bxh2+! 21. Qxh2 Qxh2+ 22. Kxh2 cxd3 23. Rc5 Rab8 $17) 16... Rb8! $36) (14... Nf4!? 15. Bxf4 Bxf4 16. g3 Be5 17. Rac1 (17. f4? Bd4+ 18. Kg2 Rb8 $17) 17... Bd4 18. Rc2 e5 { Black has a strong bishop on d4, and the knight on a4 is inactive. Black can sacrifice the pawn on c6 and play for compensation. } 19. Rfc1 { [%csl Yd4] } 19... Qd8!? 20. Kg2 (20. Qe2 Qg5 21. Kg2 Qh6 $36) 20... Qd7 21. b3 (21. Rxc6? Bxf2 $17 { [%cal Gd7d3] }) 21... Rb8 $36) 15. b3! Rd8!? (15... Be5?! 16. Bb2! $14) (15... Nf4 16. Bxf4 Bxf4 17. g3 Be5 18. Rac1! Bd4 19. Rc2 g6 20. Qe2 h5 $13) 16. Rd1 Rb4 $132 { One of the critical positions in the game has arisen. Ju took 20 minutes to think and played a really interesting move by sacrificing a pawn. } (16... Nf4 { Black always could do this, but as Tan said in the post-mortem analysis, she wanted to maintain the tension and play for the win, as she was 2 points behind in the match. } 17. Bxf4 Bxf4 18. Bf1) 17. Bb2!? (17. Bg5! Rd7 18. Be3 Nf4 19. Bxf4 Bxf4 20. Nc5 Rdd4 21. Bc4) 17... Nf4 18. Qf3 Nxd3 19. Rxd3 Ba6! { It is important to develop the bishop before capturing on h2. } (19... Bxh2+? 20. Kh1 Rxd3 21. Qxd3 Bd6 22. Rd1! Be7 23. Ba3 Rb8 24. Bxe7 Qxe7 25. Qd8+ Qf8 26. Qa5 $18 { [%cal Gd1d8,Ga5a7] }) 20. Rdd1 Bxh2+ 21. Kh1 Bd6 22. Qc3 (22. Ba3!? { Another fine option: White exchanges the dark-squared bishops and goes for a compensation position. } 22... Rb5 23. Bxd6 Rxd6 24. Rxd6 Qxd6 25. Rd1 Qe7 26. Kg1 h6! 27. Qc3 $44) 22... Bf8 23. Nc5?! { Provides an opportunity for Black to take the initiative. } (23. Rxd8! Qxd8 24. Ba3! Rxe4 25. Bxf8 Kxf8 26. Qxc6 Qd4! 27. Rc1 g6 28. Qc5+! (28. Qxa6?? Qxf2! $19 { [%cal Ge4h4] }) 28... Qxc5 29. Nxc5 Rh4+ 30. Kg1 Bb5 31. a4! Be8 32. Na6! $44 { [%cal Gc1c8] }) 23... Rb6?! (23... Be2! { This is a very exciting tactical move, and I really like the variation that follows. } 24. Rxd8 Qxd8 25. Kg1! (25. Qxb4? Qh4+ 26. Kg1 Bd6! { Black is simply winning! } 27. f3 (27. g3 Bxg3! 28. fxg3 Qxg3+ 29. Kh1 Bf3#) 27... Bh2+ 28. Kh1 Bg3+ 29. Kg1 Qh2#) 25... Rb5! 26. Qe3 Bxc5 27. Qxe2 Bb6!? $15) 24. Qe5 Qe7 25. Rac1? { This is a very logical move; however, it allows Black to gain a significantly better position. } (25. Rxd8! Qxd8 26. Kg1! $13) 25... Rb5?! (25... Rxd1+! { With this move, Tan would have had the biggest chance to win. The variation is pretty complex and concrete. } 26. Rxd1 Rb5 27. Bd4 (27. b4? Rxb4!! { I think this is a crazy idea that both players probably overlooked. } 28. Nxa6 f6! { A crucial intermediate move. } (28... Rb6? 29. Nc5 f6 30. Qxe6+! Qxe6 31. Nxe6 Rxb2 32. Rd8 Kf7 33. Nxf8 Rxf2! $132) 29. Qc3 (29. Qc7 Rxb2 $19) 29... Rb6! $19 { This is a fantastic position. The knight on a6 is completely trapped. }) 27... Rxc5! 28. Bxc5 Qxc5 29. Qxc5 Bxc5 30. Rd8+ Bf8 31. Ra8 Bd3 32. Rxa7 Bxe4 $17) 26. Rxd8 (26. Rd4!? $13 { This is a nice move that maintains tension in the center. }) 26... Qxd8 27. Qd4 (27. Bd4!? Bc8! 28. a4 Rb4 $15) 27... Qh4+?! (27... Qg5! { The queen is in a better position here. } { [%csl Rc5,Gg5][%cal Gf8c5] } 28. b4 e5!? (28... Bxc5 29. Rxc5 Rxc5 30. Qxc5 Qxc5 31. bxc5 Bc4 32. a3 { Black has an extra pawn, but the position appears quite drawish due to the opposite-colored bishops endgame. }) 29. Qxe5 Qd2! 30. Kg1 Rxb4 31. Nb3! Qe2 32. Bd4 Rc4 33. Ra1! h6 $36) 28. Kg1 Bc8?! (28... e5! 29. Qxe5 Qd8! { [%cal Gb5c5] } 30. Qd4 Qxd4 31. Bxd4 Rb4! 32. Be3 Bxc5 33. Rxc5 Rxe4 34. Rxc6 Bb5!) 29. e5 (29. a4!? { If Ju had played for a win, this move should have been played, but the match situation allowed her to play for a solid draw. } 29... Rb8 30. Qe5! Ra8 31. Qc7 Qg5! 32. Qxc6 Rb8 33. Rd1 e5! $13) 29... Qxd4 30. Bxd4 a5 31. f4 h5 32. Kf2 { Of course, White is activating her king. } 32... Be7 (32... Bxc5 { This is usually good for a draw, but Tan is still playing for more?! }) (32... Kh7!? { I like this move. Black also aims to activate her King as well. } 33. Ke3 Kg6) 33. Ke3 g5?! (33... Bxc5 { Although the simplest move leads to a draw, Tan likely played for a win but misjudged her chances. } 34. Rxc5 Bb7) 34. Ne4! gxf4+ 35. Kxf4 Bb7?? { This is a wrong move. Black overlooks White's strong move to exchange the dark-squared bishops, after which the position is very hard to defend. The main problem for Black here is the weak light-squared bishop. } (35... a4!? { Not an easy move, but Black needs to simplify. } 36. Rxc6 (36. bxa4?! Rb4 $36) (36. Bc5 Bxc5 37. Nxc5 axb3 38. axb3 Kg7 39. Kg5 Ra5 40. Kxh5 Ra2 $13 { [%cal Ga2g2,Gc8a6] }) 36... Bb7 37. Rb6 Rxb6 38. Bxb6 axb3 39. axb3 h4) 36. Bc5! $18 { The key and decisive move of the game. After trading the bishops, the strong knight against the bad bishop on b7 is quite sad for Black. } 36... Bxc5 (36... Bd8 37. Rd1! $18) (36... Kf8!? { More stubborn. } 37. Rc3!? Bxc5 38. Nxc5 a4!? 39. bxa4 Rb4+ 40. Kg5 Bc8 41. Rc2 $18 { [%cal Gg5h5] }) 37. Nxc5! Kg7? (37... a4!? { Black needs to make this move because it provides some counterplay opportunities. } 38. Kg5! axb3 39. axb3 Kg7 40. Rc4! Bc8 41. b4?? { With full domination. }) 38. Kg5 (38. a4!? Rb4+ 39. Kg5 $18) 38... Ba8 39. Kxh5 Rb8 40. Rc4! $18 Rh8+ 41. Kg4 $18 { After reaching the 40th move, players got extra time on the clock, and it was evident that Ju was on track to win. White has a substantial advantage, with a powerful knight on c5 effectively dominating a poorly positioned bishop on a8. The position was grim for Black, and Ju skillfully converted her advantage into a victory. } 41... Rh2 42. Kg3 Rh5 43. Rg4+ Kf8 44. Ra4 Ke7 45. Rxa5 Rxe5 46. Ra7+ Kd6 47. b4! Kd5 { 1-0 White wins. } 1-0


