1. e4 { The first big surprise of the game is that Tan has played 1.e4 this time. In all her previous games, she had opted for 1.c4. By switching to a different opening, she aims to complicate the situation, especially since she is in a must-win scenario. } 1... e5 { It was quite predictable that Ju would opt for the solid 1...e5 move. } 2. Nc3 { Tan seemed to prefer avoiding the solid Petroff defense and opted for less explored territory. } (2. Nf3 Nf6!?) 2... Nf6 3. Bc4 (3. Nf3 Nc6 4. Bb5 Bb4 5. O-O O-O 6. d3 Bxc3 7. bxc3 d6 $13 { The position is similar to the game, but the bishop is on c4. }) 3... Nc6 4. d3 Bb4!? { This is a solid response, and I would say it is Giri's favorite. The key point is to pin the knight and sometimes play for d7-d5. } (4... Bc5 { This is likely to be transposed into the Italian variation. } 5. Nf3) (4... Na5 { This is considered the main move. }) 5. Nf3 (5. Ne2 d5! { This is one of the points behind b4. } { [%cal Gb4e1] } 6. exd5 Nxd5 7. O-O Be6! 8. Bxd5 Bxd5 9. f4 f6!? $132 { Black is fine here. }) 5... d6 (5... d5 6. exd5 Nxd5 7. O-O!? { I believe this tactical line is what Tan was looking for. } 7... Bxc3 8. bxc3 Bg4! (8... Nxc3? 9. Qe1! Nd5 10. Ba3! $18 { [%cal Ga3f8] }) 9. Re1 O-O 10. Bd2! $13 { [%cal Gh2h3,Ga2a4] }) 6. O-O Bxc3! { I like this approach. Black is capturing on c3, which eliminates White's option to play ♘d5. It’s important to note that the position is very similar to the Four Knights Variation (1.e4 e5 2.♘f3 ♘c6 3.♘c3 ♘f6 4.♗b5 ♗b4); the main difference is the bishop on b5. However, the underlying ideas remain quite similar. } 7. bxc3 O-O 8. Bg5 (8. h3 { It is relatively harmless. } 8... h6 9. Re1 Na5 10. Bb3 c5!? $132) 8... h6 { Including the h7-h6 move is usually beneficial. The back rank is now secure, and Black also has the option to move g7-g5. } 9. Bh4 Na5!? (9... g5?? { Certainly, this is not a move that Black should make. } 10. Nxg5! hxg5 11. Bxg5 Kg7 12. f4! $18) (9... Bg4?! 10. h3 Bh5 11. g4 Bg6 12. Nd2 $16) (9... Qe7 { Here is an alternative option that usually transposes to the game. } 10. Re1 Na5 11. Bb3 Nxb3 12. axb3 { - 9...♘a5 }) 10. Bb3 Nxb3 11. axb3 { After removing the light-squared bishop, Black has a clever way to unpin the knight on f6, which is typical in this structure. The plan is to play ♕e7-e6. } 11... Qe7! 12. Re1 (12. c4 Bg4 13. h3 Bxf3 14. Qxf3 g5 15. Bg3 { (Anton Guijarro-Abdusattorov, chess24.com 2022) } 15... a5!? $132 { [%cal Gb7b6,Gf6d7,Gd7c5] }) (12. h3 Qe6 13. Re1 b6 $132) 12... Qe6 { A logical move, but Black has different setup options as well. No wonder Ju has thought for 15 minutes about this move; she wanted to find the right plan. } (12... Bg4!? { Giving up the light-squared bishop and then continuing with g7-g5 and a7-a5 is also a very solid approach for Black. } 13. h3 Bxf3 14. Qxf3 g5 15. Bg3 a5 $132) 13. d4!? { Tan is playing quite fast, having used only 10 minutes from the clock. Her position seems preferable; however, Ju has found a solid and accurate move that keeps the balance. } 13... Nd7!! { I find this clever idea quite impressive. It is rather a multiple purpose move. First, it opens the 6th rank for the black queen, also creates the opportunity to play f7-f6 or f7-f5. Additionally, it controls the c5 square as well. } 14. Qd3 (14. d5 Qg6 $132 { [%cal Gf7f5] }) (14. c4 f6! { It was likely Ju's idea. The pawn structure is healthy, and the position is Okay for Black. } 15. d5 (15. Bg3 b6 $132) 15... Qf7 { [%cal Ga7a5,Gb7b6] }) 14... a6 { I suppose the main idea is to control the b5 square and prepare for b7-b5. } (14... f6 { This looks more also straightforward. } { [%cal Ge6f7] } 15. Bg3 Qf7 16. Nh4 Nb8!! $132 { What a move! In a closed position, Black can allow himself such a strange-looking move. The point is to open up the c8-h3 diagonal for the bishop and also prepares for ♘c6. }) 15. Rad1?! { It's a standard move, but it's inaccurate. More aggressive plans would be preferable. } (15. Bg3!? { I prefer this plan. The idea is ♘h4-f5. } { [%cal Gf3h4,Gh4f5] } 15... f6 16. Nh4 { [%cal Gh4f5,Gf2f4] } 16... Re8 17. Nf5 Nf8 $132) 15... Re8!? 16. Bg3 b5?! { It controls the c4 square, but at some point, White can play c3-c4, and the b5 pawn can be a hook pawn. } (16... f6! { I like this move again. It connects with the ♕f7 plan. It's clear that the g6 square is weakened, but White cannot exploit it. } 17. Nh4 Nf8 18. Nf5 Qf7 $132) 17. Nd2?! { Tan is starting to pursue a misguided plan. } (17. Ra1!? { The rook stands well on a1. } 17... Bb7 18. d5 Qe7 19. c4!? $14) (17. h3!? $14 { I would say this useful move is typical of Karpov. White is slightly improving her position and not giving Black counter-chances. }) 17... Nf6! { The knight has moved from f3, so the e5 pawn is no longer hanging. Ju wisely uses the time to regroup her knight from d7 and prepare ♘h5. } 18. b4 { Tan took around twenty minutes to play this move. Finding the right plan for White is challenging, as Black's position is quite stable. From this point, Ju is outplaying her opponent move by move. } (18. d5 Qe7 19. c4 Bd7! $132) (18. f3!? Nh5 19. Nf1 Qg6 $132) 18... Nh5! 19. Nb3 (19. h3) 19... Qg6 (19... f6!? $36 { [%cal Ge6f7] }) 20. dxe5 Nxg3 21. hxg3 Rxe5 $132 { The target is the e4 pawn. The position is equal, but is much easier to handle with black. } 22. Na5 Bd7 23. Re3?! (23. f4! { The best is. } 23... Re7 24. Re3 Rae8 25. Rde1 $132) 23... Rae8?! (23... f5! $36 { Black already could take the initiative with this move. }) 24. Rde1 f5! 25. Qd4? { It's a mistake. White is sacrificing the pawn in the wrong direction. } (25. exf5! { White needs to go to this line. } 25... Bxf5 (25... Rxe3 26. Rxe3! Rxe3 27. Qxe3 Bxf5 28. Nc6! $132) 26. Qd2! Rxe3 27. Rxe3 Rxe3 28. Qxe3 Bxc2 29. Qe7! { White has sufficient compensation for the pawn, thanks to the active queen, leading to a draw. }) 25... Rxe4! 26. Rxe4 Rxe4 27. Rxe4 fxe4 28. c4? { Tan overlooked the strong response from Black. } (28. Qa7! { The best option. } 28... Kh7! (28... e3? 29. Qxe3 Qxc2 30. Kh2!! { The king is safe here. } 30... Qg6 31. Qa7! $132) 29. Nb3!! { It is essential to regroup the knight. } (29. Qxc7? e3!! { What a nice idea! } 30. fxe3 (30. Qxd7 e2 31. Qe7 Qxc2 32. Kh2 d5!! { Threatening e4, and taking away the e4 square from the white queen. } { [%csl Ge4] } 33. f3 Qd1 $19) 30... Bf5! $19) 29... e3 30. Qxe3 Qxc2 31. Nd4 Qd1+ 32. Kh2 Qg4 $36) 28... e3! { Ju has found this clever move that opens the diagonal for the black queen. Black is now winning. } { [%cal Gg6c2] } 29. Qxe3 Qxc2 $19 30. Qa7 bxc4 31. Qxc7 Bb5! { The c-pawn is very powerful, and Black is clearly winning. } 32. Nb7 c3 33. Nxd6 Qd3 34. Qf7+ Kh7 35. Nf5 Qd7! { Yes, of course. Black is trading queens after easily winning the position. } 36. Qxd7 Bxd7 37. Nd4 Ba4! 38. Ne2 c2 39. f3 Kg6! 40. Kf2 Bb5 41. Nc1 h5!? { What a nice technique from Ju! It's really impressive. } 42. Ke3 Kg5 43. Kd2 Bf1! 44. Nb3 Bxg2 45. Nd4 Bxf3!? { Oh, yes, she is also playing for beauty! :) Black's pawns are very strong. } 46. Nxf3+ Kg4 47. Nd4 Kxg3 48. Nf5+ Kg4 49. Ne3+ Kf3 50. Nxc2 h4 51. Ne1+ Kg3 52. Ke3 h3 53. Nf3 h2 { 0-1 Black wins. } 0-1


