{ After achieving four consecutive victories, Ju only needed a draw to defend her title as World Women's Champion. I anticipated that she would play solidly in the final match with the goal of securing a draw. The game remained largely balanced and ultimately ended in a peaceful draw.  In the end, Ju Wenjun successfully defended her title after nine games, finishing with an impressive score of 6.5 to 2.5. }
1. e4 { Yes, every game Ju has played this move. } 1... c5 { Once again, we encounter the Sicilian. } 2. Nf3 Nc6 (2... e6 { Tan has played this move three times, but after round 6, she changed it to 2...♘c6. }) 3. Bb5 { In round 7, Ju was successful with this move and eventually won the game. However, during the opening and early middlegame stages, Tan had opportunities to seize a strong initiative. Therefore, it was predictable for both players to repeat this line. Additionally, the Rossolimo is a solid choice of opening. } 3... e6 4. O-O Nge7 5. Re1 { Ju has prepared this main move for this game. The main idea of this move is to clear the f1 square for the bishop, and then play for c3-d4 to establish a strong, stable center. } (5. d4 { This was played in Round 7. }) 5... Nd4 (5... a6 { This is the main move, but logically, Tan wanted to try something less popular, hoping to confuse Ju a bit. } 6. Bf1 d5 7. exd5 Nxd5 8. d4! Nf6 9. Be3 cxd4 10. Nxd4 $14) 6. Nxd4 cxd4 7. c3! { A strong move that has been played many times before; Ju played it immediately. White is undermining the central pawn. } 7... a6 (7... Nc6 8. d3!? Bc5 9. Nd2 $14 { [%cal Gd2f3] }) 8. Bf1! { The bishop is safe here. } 8... Nc6 9. Na3 { White is regrouping the knight to c2, where it applies more pressure on the d4 pawn. } (9. b4! { Choosing a more ambitious move would involve greater risk for White if she needed to play for a win. However, the continuation of the game is more solid and absolutely correct given the match situation. } 9... Be7 (9... Bd6!? { I assume this was Tan's preparation. Caruana has won against Cheparinov with Black in the last Olympiad, so the line is better for White, but it is also totally double-edged. } 10. Bb2 dxc3 11. dxc3 Qc7 12. h3 O-O 13. Nd2 $14) 10. a4 O-O 11. Na3 dxc3 12. dxc3 Bf6 13. Bb2 d5 14. a5 $14) 9... Be7 (9... Bc5 { More popular. } 10. Nc2 O-O 11. b4 Ba7 12. cxd4 Nxd4 13. Bb2 Nxc2 14. Qxc2) 10. Nc2 d5! { Tan has made this exact move right away. The position appears quite balanced and likely to lead to a draw. I believe that in a few games, Tan would need to focus on solid play to secure a draw, which could make the match much closer. However, I understand that it's easy to make these observations in hindsight. } 11. Nxd4 { After 8 minutes of thought, White is choosing the clearest path to maintain balance and strive for equality. } 11... Nxd4 12. cxd4 dxe4 13. d3! { White makes a precise move, opening the c1 diagonal while removing the central pawns. } 13... exd3 (13... Qxd4 14. dxe4 Qxd1 15. Rxd1 Bd7 16. e5 Bc6 17. g3 { [%cal Gc1e3,Gf1g2] }) 14. Bxd3! { Following up with ♗e4 is an accurate way to play. } (14. Qxd3 O-O 15. Qe4 Bf6 16. Be3 Ra7! $132 { [%cal Gb7b6,Ga7d7,Gc8b7] }) 14... O-O (14... Bd7 15. d5! { I am pretty sure Ju would use the fact that the Black king hasn't castled yet. } 15... O-O! (15... exd5? 16. Qh5! $16 { A fabulous multi-purpose move. The queen fantastically stands on h5. } { [%csl Rh7][%cal Gh5d5,Gc1g5] } 16... Bc6) 16. Qc2 exd5 17. Bxh7+ Kh8 18. Bd3) 15. Be4! Bf6?! (15... Bg5 16. Bxg5 Qxg5 17. Qc2 h6 18. Rad1 $14) (15... h6 { It's more accurate now. The goal is to prepare for ♗g5, and the h7 pawn is no longer hanging. }) 16. Be3 g6 (16... h6 17. Rc1 $14) 17. Qb3 { After 13 minutes of thought, Ju played this forcing move. The point is to simplify the position. 17.c1 would be more precise, and White has better prospects after that. } (17. Rc1! Bg5 18. g3! Bxe3 19. Rxe3 $14 { White has a stronger bishop on e4 and can launch an attack on the kingside with h4-h5. Black struggles to utilize the isolated pawn on d4 effectively. Overall, White enjoys a favorable position. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gh2h4,Gh4h5] }) 17... Bxd4 18. Rad1 e5 19. Bxd4 exd4 20. Qb4 a5 21. Qxd4 { The position is quite symmetrical and well-balanced. It is likely that a draw can be easily achieved. } 21... Qxd4 22. Rxd4 Be6 23. Bd5!? Rfd8 24. Red1 Rac8 { The last trick. White just needs to focus a bit more, as her back rank is weak. Both moves, ♔f1 or g2-g3, make sense. Then the position is a dead draw. } 25. Kf1 (25. Bxe6?? Rxd4 26. Rxd4 Rc1+ 27. Rd1 Rxd1#) (25. g3) 25... Rd6 26. Bxe6 Rxe6 27. Rd8+ Rxd8 28. Rxd8+ Kg7 29. Rd7 b6 30. g4 Kf6 31. Rb7 h5 32. gxh5 gxh5 33. Kg2 Kg6 34. Kg3 f6 35. h4 Kf5 36. Rh7 Kg6 37. Rb7 Kf5 38. Rh7 Kg6 { We witnessed a peaceful draw in a thrilling battle between the two Chinese superstars. The match concluded earlier than expected, as Ju reached 6.5 points after nine games. Anish Giri remarked that the match reminded him of the World Championship match between Nepomniachtchi and Carlsen.  Overall, Ju was mentally more prepared, as she certainly has much more experience. She played solidly and thoughtfully throughout the games.  The matches were quite lengthy, and I commend both players for their efforts. Congratulations to Ju Wenjun for successfully defending her World Championship title! } 1/2-1/2


